    tags: ['emancipation', 'reconstruction', 'texas', 'memory', 'community'],
    summary: `Juneteenth marks a delay that should make readers uncomfortable. The Emancipation Proclamation took effect on January 1, 1863, but enslaved people in Texas were not told by federal troops that slavery had ended there until June 19, 1865, when Union General Gordon Granger issued General Order No. 3 in Galveston. The day became a Black freedom celebration rooted in Texas and carried across the country through family, church, food, music, migration, organizing, and memory. Juneteenth matters because it refuses the easy version of emancipation. Freedom did not arrive everywhere at once. It had to be announced, enforced, claimed, celebrated, and defended.`,
    timeline: [
      { date: 'Jan 1, 1863', event: 'The Emancipation Proclamation takes effect for enslaved people in Confederate-held areas.' },
      { date: 'Apr 9, 1865', event: 'Confederate General Robert E. Lee surrenders at Appomattox.' },
      { date: 'Jun 2, 1865', event: 'Confederate forces west of the Mississippi surrender in Galveston, Texas.' },
      { date: 'Jun 19, 1865', event: 'General Gordon Granger issues General Order No. 3 in Galveston.' },
      { date: 'Dec 6, 1865', event: 'The Thirteenth Amendment is ratified, abolishing slavery except as punishment for crime.' },
      { date: '1866', event: 'Black Texans begin annual Juneteenth celebrations.' },
      { date: '1872', event: 'Black Houstonians help purchase land for Emancipation Park.' },
      { date: '1980', event: 'Texas makes Juneteenth an official state holiday.' },
      { date: 'Jun 17, 2021', event: 'Juneteenth National Independence Day becomes a federal holiday.' }
    ],
    fullStory: [
      `Juneteenth begins with a gap. Abraham Lincoln issued the Emancipation Proclamation in 1862, and it took effect on January 1, 1863, but the proclamation depended on war, Union military power, and actual enforcement. It did not instantly free every enslaved person, and it did not reach every place in the same way. Texas, geographically distant and still under Confederate influence, became one of the last places where enslaved people heard freedom announced by federal troops.`,
      `On June 19, 1865, Union General Gordon Granger issued General Order No. 3 in Galveston. The order stated that the people of Texas were informed, in accordance with a proclamation from the U.S. executive, that all enslaved people were free. That announcement did not mean every enslaver complied willingly. Freedom had to move from words to labor contracts, movement, family searches, land struggles, education, political organizing, and protection from violence.`,
      `The order's language also carried the limits of its moment. It told formerly enslaved people to remain at their present homes and work for wages, and it warned that they would not be supported in idleness. That sentence shows how emancipation arrived braided with control. The government announced freedom while also trying to manage Black labor and movement. Formerly enslaved people heard freedom and immediately had to negotiate what freedom would mean against employers, courts, soldiers, and white violence.`,
      `Black Texans began celebrating the anniversary in 1866. Juneteenth gatherings included prayer, food, music, dress, speeches, political education, family reunions, and public joy. The celebrations were not escapes from politics. They were politics. At a time when white Texans often tried to restrict Black public assembly, Black communities created and defended spaces to remember emancipation on their own terms.`,
      `Land mattered. In Houston, Black residents purchased land that became Emancipation Park in the 1870s because public celebration needed a place. That act of buying land for memory shows how Juneteenth was built through institution-making, not only feeling. Churches, schools, benevolent groups, and families carried the holiday forward.`,
      `As Black Texans migrated, Juneteenth moved too. The holiday became part of community life beyond Texas, though it did not always receive national attention. During parts of the twentieth century, observance varied as migration, school calendars, work schedules, assimilation pressures, and civil rights politics reshaped Black public life. But the holiday never disappeared. It survived because people kept cooking, gathering, teaching, singing, praying, and telling the story.`,
      `The civil rights and Black Power eras helped renew wider attention to Juneteenth. The Poor People's Campaign and later Black cultural movements connected the holiday to broader claims about freedom, economic justice, and self-determination. Texas made Juneteenth a state holiday in 1980, after years of advocacy including work by state legislator Al Edwards.`,
      `The holiday's foods, clothes, sermons, parades, and family gatherings also carried meaning. Red foods and drinks are often connected by cultural historians to memory, celebration, sacrifice, and African diasporic foodways. Dress mattered because formerly enslaved people and their descendants used public celebration to claim dignity in a society that had tried to control Black bodies and Black assembly. Juneteenth was history taught through the senses.`,
      `In 2021, Juneteenth became a federal holiday after the racial justice protests of 2020 and a wider public reckoning with anti-Black violence. Federal recognition mattered, but it also raised questions. Would the holiday become a substitute for policy? Would it be commercialized? Would its history be taught honestly, including Reconstruction, racial terror, labor exploitation, and the long fight over voting rights?`,
      `Juneteenth matters because it is both celebration and evidence. It celebrates Black survival, family, food, joy, and freedom. It also proves that freedom written on paper can be delayed, narrowed, ignored, or stolen unless people force it into daily life.`
    ],
    quote: {
      text: `The people of Texas are informed that all slaves are free.`,
      cite: 'General Order No. 3, June 19, 1865, National Archives'
    },
    costImpact: [
      `The cost behind Juneteenth was generations of slavery, delayed enforcement, coerced labor after emancipation, family separation, landlessness, racial terror, and the long struggle to make legal freedom real. The announcement in Galveston did not repair what slavery had stolen, and it did not prevent new systems of exploitation from forming.`,
      `The impact was also profound. Juneteenth gave Black communities a calendar of their own, a way to remember freedom from the perspective of those who had been denied it. It sustained local institutions, public memory, political education, and joy across generations before the federal government recognized it.`
    ],
    whyItMattersToday: [
      `Juneteenth matters today because it teaches the difference between declaration and delivery. Laws can change before lives change. Rights can exist on paper while officials, employers, courts, and mobs block them in practice.`,
      `It also challenges shallow celebrations of freedom. Juneteenth is not only a summer holiday or a branding opportunity. It is a reminder that Black communities preserved memory when the nation ignored it, and that celebration can be a serious form of historical work.`,
      `Readers should understand Juneteenth as joy with a spine: food, music, family, and public gathering anchored in the knowledge that freedom had to be fought into reality and still has to be defended.`
    ],
